<section id="conclusion">
  <h2>Conclusion and Next Steps</h2>

  <p>
    In this work, we developed a standardized preprocessing and retrieval pipeline for
    time-series event retrieval and validated it on the MIT-BIH ECG dataset. The pipeline
    successfully converts raw signals into fixed-length, metadata-aligned samples and supports
    both time-domain and frequency-domain representations in a unified format.
  </p>

  <p>
    Our experiments provide several important insights. First, time-domain representations
    consistently outperform frequency-domain representations for ECG retrieval tasks, as they
    preserve waveform morphology such as the P wave, QRS complex, and T wave. Second, the choice
    of windowing strategy plays a critical role. A context-aware window centered around the R-peak
    significantly improves retrieval quality compared to shorter, isolated segments. Finally,
    preliminary results suggest that representation quality has a larger impact on retrieval
    performance than embedding complexity in early-stage systems.
  </p>

  <p>
    These findings indicate that, for physiological signals such as ECG, similarity is primarily
    driven by temporal structure rather than spectral characteristics. As a result, preserving
    local waveform information is essential for effective event retrieval.
  </p>

  <h3>Limitations</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Experiments were conducted on a single dataset (MIT-BIH ECG)</li>
    <li>Embedding methods have not yet been fully explored or optimized</li>
    <li>Cross-domain generalization has not been evaluated at this stage</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Next Steps</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Extend experiments to multiple datasets (e.g., IMU, vibration, UCR archive)</li>
    <li>Evaluate advanced embedding methods such as TS2Vec, InceptionTime, and tsfresh</li>
    <li>Incorporate time-frequency representations (e.g., STFT) for richer signal encoding</li>
    <li>Perform systematic hyperparameter tuning across representations and embeddings</li>
    <li>Evaluate cross-domain generalization and identify robust retrieval configurations</li>
    <li>Integrate scalable vector search systems (e.g., HNSW, Faiss) for large-scale evaluation</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Overall, this work establishes a strong foundation for systematic evaluation of time-series
    event retrieval. Future work will focus on expanding the framework across domains and identifying
    representation and embedding strategies that generalize reliably.
  </p>

</section>
